reformulations.equivalence_argument:
For entire f, `iteratedDeriv n f 0 / n!` are the Taylor coefficients (Mathlib `Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_of_entire`); the complex derivative is used because the domain of f is ℂ. Proved in Lean (scratch file), for every f with `Differentiable ℂ f`:
(i) the circle family `‖f z‖`, ‖z‖ = r, is bounded above;
(ii) each term ‖a_n r^n‖ ≤ ⨆_{|z|=r} ‖f z‖ for r > 0 (Cauchy's estimate);
(iii) 0 ≤ ratio r f ≤ 1 for r > 0;
(iv) 0 ≤ liminf (ratio · f) atTop ≤ 1;
(v) the outer family is bounded above by 1, and the ⨆ is ≤ 1.
The numerator is a genuine max, since |a_n| r^n → 0. So ratio = μ(r)/M(r) for r > 0 (M(r) > 0 because f ≢ 0), the liminf is genuine, and the outer ⨆ is the genuine supremum B once the subtype is nonempty. Nonemptiness holds classically: exp is not algebraic over ℂ[z]. That step was not proved in Lean.
